APPENDIX A — ADJACENCY AS ROUGH APPROXIMATION
============================================================
  Adjacency provides a rough combinatorial scaffold for I,
  but cannot define the relational horizon.  Only closure-
  geodesic distances dΓ (or discounted dΓ^(λ)) correctly
  capture the breakdown boundary where dq fails and Q-box
  interior begins.

ADJACENCY DISTANCE (COMBINATORIAL)
----------------------------------
  d_adj(x,y) = minimal number of adjacency steps across refinements

PROPERTIES
----------
  - Purely combinatorial
  - No geometry, no coherence density
  - No Γ-flow, no closure geodesics
  - Valid only far from Q-box interior

RELATION TO dΓ
--------------
  d_adj(x,y)  ≈  rough upper bound on dΓ(x,y)
  d_adj(x,y)  ≠  substitute for dΓ(x,y)

BREAKDOWN NEAR HORIZON
----------------------
  As x → H:
    - Γ-flow becomes highly non-uniform
    - adjacency neighborhoods distort
    - d_adj loses correlation with dΓ
    - coordinate metric dq diverges
    - relational metric dΓ remains finite

USE CASE
--------
  d_adj is acceptable for:
    - coarse exploration of I
    - rough neighborhood estimates
    - debugging refinement structures

  d_adj is NOT acceptable for:
    - defining h[x]
    - locating H
    - measuring relational thickness
    - any Γ-consistent geometry
